alt-syscall: allow fchmodat2 in "complete"

fchmodat2 (452, Linux 6.6) is the flags-taking fchmodat, and a current
glibc tries it first for fchmodat(AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW) and lchmod(),
falling back to fchmodat (or O_PATH + /proc) on -ENOSYS. Under the
enforcing "complete" table it hit block_syscall(), so nothing broke but
every call logged "blocked syscall 452" -- seen from the Notialis
container on rpi5-openfyde ("trans_run2: blocked syscall 452").

It is the same permission check as fchmodat, which both lists already
allow, so it goes in next to faccessat2 in the shared 64-bit list (452 on
x86_64 and arm64 alike) and next to fchmodat in the compat list (452 on
i386 and ARM EABI too).

--- v6.12-rpi.orig/security/chromiumos/complete_whitelists.h	2026-10-16 12:56:29.449761166 +0800
+++ v6.12-rpi/security/chromiumos/complete_whitelists.h	2026-10-16 12:56:29.458761318 +0800
@@ -63,6 +63,7 @@
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(exit_group),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(faccessat),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(faccessat2),
+	SYSCALL_ENTRY(fchmodat2),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(fallocate),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(fanotify_init),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(fanotify_mark),
@@ -459,6 +460,7 @@
 	COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY(fchdir),
 	COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY(fchmod),
 	COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY(fchmodat),
+	COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY(fchmodat2),
 	COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY(fchown),
 	COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY(fchown32),
 	COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY(fchownat),
